From: John P Speno <speno () isc ! upenn ! edu>
Date: Fri, 29 Sep 2000 13:42:45 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: FYI: Flaw in KGPSA (Emulex Fibre Channel Host Bus Adapters)
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=97023489925146

We just received this over the wire from our friendly VAR:

Compaq Computer Corporation has identified a flaw in the Emulex LP8000 64     
bit PCI Fibre Channel Host Bus Adapter. The failure occurs in a small         
percentage of the product (Compaq SKUs  DS-KGPSA-CA (168794-B21) and          
DS-KGPSA-CB (176479-B21)) in environments where the product is used in the    
64 bit PCI slot under high I/O loads. The failure could result in invalid     
data returned to the host on reads from the disk or tape.
                                                                              
Extensive follow up testing indicates that the problem is independent of any  
server platform. Compaq has identified the problem at a single customer site  
to date, but has been able to replicate the failure in specific lab tests.    
While the failure occurrence is rare, Compaq regards any potential problem    
with data integrity as serious.
                                                                              
For product already installed at the customer site, a firmware upgrade that   
corrects the potential for the blem occurring is  available at the Compaq     
website below:                                                                
                                                                              
http://www.compaq.com/products/storageworks/adapters/64bitpcifibre.html       
                                                                              
This is available from Compaq Customer Services as well.  Complete            
instructions for installing the upgrade, which will require powering down     
the server, will be provided.                                                 
                                                                              
Compaq has begun to notify customers of the problem and the resolution.

